<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch04_P02_Best_summary</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Which is the best summary of the proof in the example above, that \(n^2 - 1\) is divisible by 8 for every odd number \(n\)?</p>
<div>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>A good summary names the structure and the key step: write the odd number as \(2k + 1\), factorise \(n^2 - 1\) as \(4k(k + 1)\), and use the fact that one of two consecutive integers is even to get a second factor 2. That is the second option.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>Checking five values is the rough work, not the proof: it cannot cover every odd number.</p></li>
<li><p>A multiple of 4 need not be a multiple of 8: 12 is not. The proof needs the extra factor 2 from \(k(k + 1)\).</p></li>
<li><p>The parity argument was the first attempt in the rough work, and it was abandoned: an even number need not be a multiple of 8 (2, 4, 6, 10, …).</p></li>
</ul>]]></text></generalfeedback>
